      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to demonstrate the feasibility of using samples obtained through muscle biopsy to assess a wide range of cellular properties, some of which may be abnormal in myalgia. Given the recent emphasis on the role of excitation-contraction coupling in health and disease, special emphasis is given to the characterization of the properties involved in this process."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Tissue samples were obtained from the upper portion of the descending trapezius muscle in three female patients (PAT) with clinically diagnosed myalgia and assessed for a spectrum of properties related to substrate use, energy production, and excitation-contraction coupling and were compared with samples from three healthy controls."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "At the level of organization of the metabolic pathways, all PAT generally displayed normal activities of enzymes representing the potential for oxidative phosphorylation, glucose phosphorylation, glycolysis, and lactate oxidation. In contrast, a reduced potential was observed in PAT for both fat oxidation (-20%) and high-energy phosphate transfer (-38%). For excitation-contraction coupling, PAT had a compromised sarcoplasmic reticulum maximal Ca-ATPase activity (-21%), Ca uptake (-44%), and sarcoplasmic endopleasmic reticulum (SERCA) expression for both SERCA1a (-16%) and SERCA2a (-17%), which were accompanied by a lower phase 2 Ca release (-45%). The Na-K-ATPase concentration, the enzyme-regulating membrane excitability via active Na and K seemed elevated (+25%) in PAT."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These results demonstrate the feasibility of analyzing tissue samples for a wide range of properties and provide a rationale for studies examining the cellular basis of myalgia with particular emphasis on sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca cycling, given the latter's role in regulating a wide range of cellular functions."
        }
      ]},
